i found out ultimately by a phone call  on a monday evening in spring and uh i  hadn't thought i'd been selected as  national i'd gone all the way through  the selection process done the final  interviews with the director general of  visa himself um and was kind of told  sure we'll let you know in a couple of  weeks a couple of weeks have come and  gone and the next thing i saw on the  internet was that the european space  agency is announcing its new core of  astronauts on wednesday this was monday  i hadn't heard anything so i hadn't been  chosen  so i was very surprised on monday night  to get the phone call literally saying  we'd like you to become an astronaut and  can you make it to paris on wednesday it  was one of the dg's staff who made the  call and i was actually out in the  garden at the time because it was a nice  spring evening and my wife came out to  meet me because she saw me on the phone  and knew that you know there was a  possibility of an important phone call  coming and so um she could tell by my  expression and the conversation that it  was obviously good news so it's one of  those moments in your life when you  realize you're making you know a huge  change for many different reasons in  terms of where you're going to live and  your future and obviously becoming an  astronaut is a massive career change and  all the responsibility that goes along  with that as well  and i think all of that that enormity  kind of hits you in one moment and  there's a whole plethora of different  emotions you're delighted and you're  excited but also you know you're you've  got some apprehension about the future  where we're going to live and  rebecca was pregnant at the time with  our first child so what's the what's the  impact on the family going to be all of  these things run through your head but  we just took them one thing at a time we  had had a serious discussion about this  just prior to the medical week during  the selection process because that's a  big week it's it's quite invasive and  you don't want to put your body through  that unless you're 100 certain about  what you're doing and also it costs a  lot of money so you don't want  the space agency to have to pay that  unless you're 100 serious that you're  going to take the job if you get offered  it so we'd had had that discussion  already and she was very supportive  and no she's the kind of character who's  always up for a challenge and  exciting futures it's i mean it's very  daunting as a rookie astronaut we spent  our initial year and a half really at  the european astronaut center and that  was just the six of my myself and my  five classmates the six of us working  together and of course we were  introduced to all the senior experienced  european astronauts so we got to talk to  them about their experiences and find  out a lot more but then when you get  assigned to a mission and you know you  come over to hear johnson space center  and also to star city and you know you  realize you're part of this enormous  machine and there's an awful lot of  pressure on you to be able to perform  and obviously there's a whole machine of  the iss program that is expecting you to  do your job very well so that can be  quite daunting but once you start the  training you realize that everybody is  extremely kind extremely supportive and  the whole system is there to set you up  for success and to make sure you can be  as good as you can be  as it happened i was in the uk when that  call came through and i was doing some  apache flying with the british army  because i i still serve as a reserve  officer and i try to get back one or two  times a year and to keep some some sort  of currency so i had just landed from a  fantastic couple of hours flying the  apache and had a call from my boss in  germany who said tim you've been  assigned to a long duration mission to  the space station  and i was i was delighted because  there had been a lot of discussion  leading up to that phone call and i knew  there was a likelihood of me being  assigned to a mission  but i thought it was probably going to  be for a short duration mission  for eight to ten days in space so to be  told no you've got a long duration  you're going for six months was i was  absolutely really delighted i was you  know really thrilled with him that's the  plum mission is that there's not  something to be said for a few short  missions as someone who loves flying and  you know  like would you rather be doing a couple  of short missions or is a long one the  plum mission i think there's a ricky  astronaut any mission's a good mission  and uh i'm not taking anything away from  the short missions because of course  before  the space station most missions were  short missions other than the russians  who were flying on the mere space  station but we had a many many shuttle  missions before that were short duration  and and several sawyers missions were  also short duration and and you can  learn an awful lot in a short duration  your training times that much shorter so  maybe in a space of three years you  could fit in you know two short duration  missions now we're moving towards these  long six-month duration missions of  living and working in space um for me i  think i i was much you know happier with  the long duration mission for me this is  a real experience to actually get to  know what it is like to spend that much  time in space  you're an astronaut and proud of being  it's a really famous job and everyone  wants to talk to you and ask you  questions and things like that what's it  like being an astronaut but having not  been in space and having you know you're  doing interviews and meeting people and  all this sort of stuff  but you can't answer these questions  that everyone has  that's a really good question and i  think i found that harder in the early  days  because especially as i i was much more  comfortable being a test pilot and that  was my expertise and i was kind of at  the the pinnacle of my career as a test  pilot very confident about what i was  doing there what i was talking about and  suddenly i'm being asked to i'm being  called an astronaut when i haven't flown  in space which seems very strange and  i'm being asked to comment on all of  these things  as i've gone through the training  process you actually gained so much more  experience about what you're training  and there are so many great things to  talk about  of what's going on here on the ground  and what we're doing i mean i've spent  12 days living under water off the coast  of florida on nasa's nemo mission um  assimilating an asteroid mission spent a  week living in a cave in sardinia  simulating a mission with other  international astronauts as well we're  training all the time with our  international partners and so the  training itself is such an experience  that there's plenty to talk about  obviously you know the icing on the cake  is the fact that you actually get to fly  to space and that's when you'll really  be able to relate your experiences to  what it's like to go on a mission the  actual mission itself is it one of these  things that it can't come quick enough i  can't wait i can't wait or because you  know it's this great moment in your life  that may not happen again it may be a  one-off but you know it's going to be  this amazing life do you almost want to  push it back a bit and sort of delay the  pleasure like how do you feel about that  um i think that that's a great question  i've never been asked that before but i  think the timing is is perfect because  um you obviously need a certain amount  of time to feel confident and to feel  prepared for your mission but at the  same token i don't want to push it any  further to the right because you know  you're not only excited about the  mission you're waiting for it to happen  but until it happens you're an unflown  astronaut and there's always a risk that  something might happen to you you might  become medically unfit um i mean let's  hope not but something could happen to  the space station that renders the  mission uh unattainable so no it's one  of those things that that it's just in  the right place i'd be ready for the  mission and you know that that's a great  time for it  i'm sure you've heard of the astronaut  prayer of you know god just please don't  let me mess up it's one of those cases  that there are there are many  opportunities where you can do things  wrong  and that's what the training is for  that's why we train for two and a half  years
 

